welcome back to cbs mornings we're  getting more reaction to las vegas  raiders head coach john gruden resigning  he stepped down amid reports he used  racist sexist and homophobic language in  a series of emails from 2010 to 2018. in  his resignation statement gruden said he  did not want to be a distraction for the  raiders he said he was sorry and never  meant to hurt anyone expatriates and  chiefs offensive lineman ryan o'callahan  is one of only few openly gay former nfl  players after coming out in 2017 he  wrote a note to his younger self for a  series note to self o'callaghan  described the pain of hiding his  sexuality while playing football  you're going to do everything in your  power to make sure no one finds out that  you're gay  you know that if your cover is blown  you'll lose everything  if you're gay you're as good as dead  you'll live 29 years of your life in  fear  you'll keep up this facade because you  think there is no alternative  powerful stuff after retiring from the  nfl ryan founded the rhino callahan  foundation to help other lgbtq athletes  he joins us for an exclusive broadcast  interview ryan good morning good morning  happy to be here thank you so let me ask  you what was your response to the emails  when you read all of that yeah when it  first came out i i looked into it and he  said some awful terrible things but what  i looked for initially was  something from john gruden i wanted to  hear what he had to say you know the  majority of these emails were a decade  ago and i'm the type of person that  believes in growth people change and  i wanted to hear specific things that  he has learned over the past decade why  his language was offensive you know  having carl  uh nasa the first openly gay player on  the team maybe that changed his  perspective about  uh lgbtq players but  he didn't have anything to say he just  you know up and  resigned um you know because of that  you know i'm sitting here the only thing  i can believe is that  he hasn't grown you know i was willing  to give him that that benefit of the  doubt because you know i regret things i  said a decade ago and i wouldn't want  those held against me because i'm a  different person  but we didn't get that from him and you  mentioned carl nasim who recently came  out and even gruden came out in support  of him as one of his players so you  believe if gruden would have came out  and owned up to it and said you know  what  that was years ago i'm not the same guy  i have a player on my roster who is gay  and we support him um you think it would  have been a different result  would he still have a job i think it  would have helped now he said a lot of  awful things about a lot of different  groups of people i think the nfl didn't  seem to be a one-off no it wasn't just  one thing a long time ago and the nfl  has come a long way in in the past  decade of trying to get in front of  these issues and actually  you know stand up for a lot of these  social justice things and even when it  wasn't popular and  it would be tough for them to have one  of their head coaches  who had said these things still be  employed it really wouldn't be a good  look so i don't know if he would have  been able to save his career  but just as a human being to come out  and and show growth i think would have  gone a long way yeah i look at your note  to self ryan uh that's where we first  met you and it it's so hard to hear you  say that at times you thought about  suicide because you knew that you  couldn't come out uh being a nfl player  do you think that the nfl has changed a  lot since then do you think it's more  open and more inclusive because you hear  emails like gruden's and you go well  has it changed how bad is it yeah  because he wasn't writing him he was  writing him to other nfl executives in  some cases he was but the nfl has a  governing body  i know they've changed i've sat across  the table from roger goodell and he's  asked me what can i do to help closeted  players and i've had these discussions  with it multiple times and  we've talked about different things  which they've then put into place and  like when carl came out the nfl was  ready  they wanted to have a better response  than what happened with you know michael  sam back in the day yes so they've taken  a lot of steps to  actually show they care and i feel  they're sincere about it some people say  this is part of cancel culture do you  see it that way  i don't only because he didn't even try  to fight it he had no defense he didn't  he didn't say you know that's not me  anymore you know so  you know if he would have stood up and  and  one own up to the awful things he said  and then talked about how he learned  from them and how he's not that person  anymore  i'd be more sympathetic to it but  because he didn't do any of that  i mean the other term for cancer culture  of course is accountability  yes we didn't hear gruden take account  for what he said we haven't heard much  from him at all he just sort of walked  away we spoke yesterday to bill uh  rhoden uh a writer sports writer uh who  talked about enablers uh gruden was  talking to a group of peers  locker rooms have cultures people say  things  how does the enabling get stopped how do  people intervene to prevent this sort of  culture from continuing i think it's a  lot easier when it's a peer-to-peer  coaching coach player to player to step  in and speak up when you hear something  that's not right but  you know for a player to  talk to your head coach about something  he said that would be more difficult but  i think players do do a good job even  back when i was playing of holding each  other accountable for the things they  say in the locker room  now i came in to know three i was  drafted by the minnesota vikings  and i can honestly say in my locker room  there wasn't homophobic slurs um being  thrown around  but my experience is different than  yours what was yours like yeah in high  school and college you had quite a bit  of that you know guys were younger and  mature but in the nfl i can't remember  one instance in the locker room of just  homophobic slurs people going on rants  it's interesting um there was a case of  a coach who would say things like no  homo which is arguably not as bad as  some of the things rudin said but still  not appropriate  but yeah i just like you i didn't have  that experience of my teammates saying  things do you think that there are a lot  of gay players in the nfl that cannot  come out or  still reluctant to come out in 2021 i  know quite a few you know quite a few  really yeah  what's it going to take  well i think carl kind of opened the  door for a lot of people you know when i  when i talk to closeted players the main  concerns are losing their job cultural  family  um and i think carl's  experience answered the question of can  you are you going to lose your job right  are they worried about the owners their  teammates or the fans  it's a societal problem too it's bigger  than sports it is the closet being in  the closet is a scary place you everyone  handles it differently but you build  this up in your mind to be something  that you don't think you can ever  conquer at least i did so  you know i don't  i don't know if it's an owners or a fan  thing but  um  you know i once again i think the nfl is  doing a good job of putting themselves  out there and saying hey you can come  out and there's a lot of owners who are  now doing things publicly to show their  support which is only going to help and  you've underscored you've underscored  something very important in this  conversation which is we have not heard  from gruden and we don't know if in his  mind he has moved on and is a different  person we want to hear what he thinks  about it today would be helpful to hear  it also good to hear that you had sat  down with roger dell who came to you and  said what can we do ryan it's so good to  see you again happy to see you joining  us  ryan's also a best-selling author just  saying by the way yeah oh by the by

